WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:03.768
- If you're going to be good at
something it does take awhile.

00:00:03.768 --> 00:00:05.856
Just enjoy the getting there.

00:00:05.856 --> 00:00:07.755
What I realized very young is that

00:00:07.755 --> 00:00:09.450
I didn't want to live in fear.

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:11.024
Where most people get it wrong

00:00:11.024 --> 00:00:13.292
is they're afraid of
losing what they've got.

00:00:13.292 --> 00:00:16.157
Anybody who at the age of
60 calls themselves Sting

00:00:16.157 --> 00:00:18.225
(audience laughs)

00:00:18.225 --> 00:00:19.225
is an idiot.

00:00:21.391 --> 00:00:23.782
- He's an English reality TV judge,

00:00:23.782 --> 00:00:25.869
entrepreneur and philanthropist.

00:00:25.869 --> 00:00:28.619
He's most recognized as
a judge on the TV series

00:00:28.619 --> 00:00:32.495
American Idol, X Factor,
and America's Got Talent.

00:00:32.495 --> 00:00:33.983
According to the Sunday Times he's one

00:00:33.983 --> 00:00:36.374
of the wealthiest people in the UK.

00:00:36.374 --> 00:00:38.444
In 2015 he had an estimated net worth

00:00:38.444 --> 00:00:40.694
of over 325 million pounds.

00:00:42.241 --> 00:00:43.687
He's Simon Cowell and here's my take

00:00:43.687 --> 00:00:45.965
on his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:45.965 --> 00:00:47.433
Rule number two is my personal favorite

00:00:47.433 --> 00:00:48.572
and make sure to stick around all the way

00:00:48.572 --> 00:00:51.115
to the end for some special bonus clips.

00:00:51.115 --> 00:00:52.670
And as always if Simon says something

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:54.355
that really, really
means something to you,

00:00:54.355 --> 00:00:56.791
is really meaningful, please
leave it in the comments below

00:00:56.791 --> 00:00:58.475
and put quotes around it so other people

00:00:58.475 --> 00:01:00.160
can be inspired as well.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:00.993
Enjoy.

00:01:04.021 --> 00:01:06.604
(upbeat music)

00:01:13.934 --> 00:01:15.670
- The good thing about how I made a career

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:17.170
was I was patient.

00:01:18.354 --> 00:01:21.015
So I would say to anybody still today,

00:01:21.015 --> 00:01:24.527
if you're going to be good at
something it does take awhile.

00:01:24.527 --> 00:01:26.875
Just enjoy the getting there.

00:01:26.875 --> 00:01:29.639
You're going to get fed up,
you're going to get disheartened

00:01:29.639 --> 00:01:31.499
and people are going to
tell you you're useless.

00:01:31.499 --> 00:01:33.059
Had all that.

00:01:33.059 --> 00:01:35.530
But what I can say after
about 15 or 20 years

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:37.512
I learned how to do something well

00:01:37.512 --> 00:01:41.419
and I enjoyed that getting
there as much as being there.

00:01:41.419 --> 00:01:43.796
- Let's say you're about
to get out of high school

00:01:43.796 --> 00:01:46.629
and you say to yourself
as you said to yourself,

00:01:46.629 --> 00:01:50.207
you know what I don't
think I'm college material.

00:01:50.207 --> 00:01:52.957
The fear sets in that number one,

00:01:54.351 --> 00:01:56.693
if you don't go off to
college then the world's

00:01:56.693 --> 00:01:58.535
going to pass you by, that's first of all.

00:01:58.535 --> 00:01:59.368
- Yeah.

00:01:59.368 --> 00:02:02.069
- Secondly it is if
something's going to happen

00:02:02.069 --> 00:02:03.673
I got to make it happen quickly.

00:02:03.673 --> 00:02:05.540
I can't afford to spend
four, five, 10 years

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:07.290
in an apprenticeship.

00:02:09.343 --> 00:02:11.175
- I think it depends on
what you want in your life.

00:02:11.175 --> 00:02:13.237
I mean what I realized very young

00:02:13.237 --> 00:02:15.302
is that I didn't want to live in fear.

00:02:15.302 --> 00:02:19.161
All my years in school I did live in fear

00:02:19.161 --> 00:02:21.275
that if you got something wrong,

00:02:21.275 --> 00:02:23.880
they would beat you I mean literally.

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:25.313
So you were kind of nervous.

00:02:25.313 --> 00:02:28.726
I was always scared
that I could have a job

00:02:28.726 --> 00:02:30.833
which I may not be very good in

00:02:30.833 --> 00:02:33.499
and my boss could hate
me and if he hated me

00:02:33.499 --> 00:02:35.962
he could fire me and I thought I couldn't

00:02:35.962 --> 00:02:40.575
live my life like that so
I kind of prepared myself

00:02:40.575 --> 00:02:42.805
to be good at something where I wouldn't

00:02:42.805 --> 00:02:45.729
have to have a boss and then
I would only have myself

00:02:45.729 --> 00:02:49.376
to answer to and then I wouldn't
live in fear any longer.

00:02:49.376 --> 00:02:52.266
And there are times in this
job where you get nervous

00:02:52.266 --> 00:02:55.571
or you lose your nerve but I
don't wake up every morning

00:02:55.571 --> 00:02:57.143
like I did when I was going to school

00:02:57.143 --> 00:03:00.305
or wake up on a Monday
morning feeling sick

00:03:00.305 --> 00:03:02.318
'cause I've got to go to school.

00:03:02.318 --> 00:03:06.617
So I'm a great believer if
you're not bright at school

00:03:06.617 --> 00:03:08.394
and I wasn't clever at school,

00:03:08.394 --> 00:03:10.608
you can teach yourself something good

00:03:10.608 --> 00:03:12.179
providing you have a good mentor

00:03:12.179 --> 00:03:14.271
and providing that you're patient.

00:03:14.271 --> 00:03:16.796
- How important is it
to you to be popular?

00:03:16.796 --> 00:03:18.342
- Very important.

00:03:18.342 --> 00:03:20.221
Whether you're a sportsman
or you're in business

00:03:20.221 --> 00:03:23.519
or you know doing what I
do where you make TV shows,

00:03:23.519 --> 00:03:26.186
nobody does this to become 77th,

00:03:27.217 --> 00:03:30.256
you know you always, always strive

00:03:30.256 --> 00:03:32.817
to be more successful
then your competition

00:03:32.817 --> 00:03:35.223
and the day that stops
you have to stop doing

00:03:35.223 --> 00:03:36.782
this for a living genuinely.

00:03:36.782 --> 00:03:38.139
You have to have that drive.

00:03:38.139 --> 00:03:40.340
Even as a kid if I was losing monopoly,

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:42.051
I'd tip the board up.

00:03:42.051 --> 00:03:42.884
(audience laughs)

00:03:42.884 --> 00:03:43.717
- Seriously?

00:03:43.717 --> 00:03:44.903
- Oh my God yeah, absolutely

00:03:44.903 --> 00:03:46.690
or I'd be the banker and nickel the money

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:48.322
so I would always win.

00:03:48.322 --> 00:03:51.482
- So tell me everybody wants
to know why are you leaving?

00:03:51.482 --> 00:03:53.958
- Probably for the same reasons you left.

00:03:53.958 --> 00:03:56.875
You just know it's time, time's up.

00:03:58.145 --> 00:04:01.593
I remember the first year when we did this

00:04:01.593 --> 00:04:04.241
and it was like an adventure

00:04:04.241 --> 00:04:08.169
and I loved that not
knowing if it was going

00:04:08.169 --> 00:04:10.954
to be successful and the buzz,

00:04:10.954 --> 00:04:12.961
we were kind of making it up every week.

00:04:12.961 --> 00:04:14.645
- Yeah when I interviewed
you after first season

00:04:14.645 --> 00:04:16.686
you said I don't know
how long this can go on,

00:04:16.686 --> 00:04:17.519
the third season--

00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:19.395
- When we first met I
remember thinking at the time

00:04:19.395 --> 00:04:20.980
this would be the one
and only time I'm sure

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:22.971
you and I'd ever meet because I'm not sure

00:04:22.971 --> 00:04:24.930
we're going to be round much longer.

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:27.347
But I just, i loved that buzz

00:04:29.183 --> 00:04:32.333
and I liked the kind of
adventure and it was fun

00:04:32.333 --> 00:04:36.441
and then you get to a point
and for whatever reason

00:04:36.441 --> 00:04:39.603
two years ago I remember
sitting in the auditions one day

00:04:39.603 --> 00:04:43.723
and I thought I think this
is about as far as we can go.

00:04:43.723 --> 00:04:45.985
And I've always promised myself

00:04:45.985 --> 00:04:49.759
that I'd leave before I was forced to go.

00:04:49.759 --> 00:04:51.641
It feels like the right thing to do.

00:04:51.641 --> 00:04:55.921
- 13 years ago you were
completely lost, gone.

00:04:55.921 --> 00:04:59.784
No money you had five pounds
and have seven dollars left

00:04:59.784 --> 00:05:03.628
in your hand, you'd use it for
a taxi back to your parents

00:05:03.628 --> 00:05:05.234
and your career in the record business

00:05:05.234 --> 00:05:08.690
had just come to a shuddering halt.

00:05:08.690 --> 00:05:10.130
And here you are 13 years later,

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:11.926
you're worth hundreds
of millions of dollars,

00:05:11.926 --> 00:05:14.016
life's taken a huge turn upwards

00:05:14.016 --> 00:05:16.641
but I get the sense with
you that what happened

00:05:16.641 --> 00:05:19.405
to you 13 years ago still defines you now.

00:05:19.405 --> 00:05:21.287
- Yeah I mean it wasn't the best of times

00:05:21.287 --> 00:05:22.851
but then again when I look back on it,

00:05:22.851 --> 00:05:26.148
it wasn't the worst of times
because I think particularly

00:05:26.148 --> 00:05:30.609
here in Hollywood where
most people get it wrong

00:05:30.609 --> 00:05:32.807
is they're afraid of
losing what they've got.

00:05:32.807 --> 00:05:34.950
They're just terrified
of losing their fame

00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:37.577
as you said power, money,

00:05:37.577 --> 00:05:40.133
but actually once you've lost it,

00:05:40.133 --> 00:05:41.372
you can always get it back again.

00:05:41.372 --> 00:05:42.485
I mean it's not the end of the world,

00:05:42.485 --> 00:05:44.726
being ill is worse.

00:05:44.726 --> 00:05:47.011
So because I've actually faced what

00:05:47.011 --> 00:05:50.618
are most people's worst
fears and recovered from it,

00:05:50.618 --> 00:05:52.372
it's not as bad as you think you know?

00:05:52.372 --> 00:05:54.557
I didn't lose my confidence.

00:05:54.557 --> 00:05:56.293
My friends were still my friends.

00:05:56.293 --> 00:05:59.824
I've lived with my parents and
I just saw it as a challenge

00:05:59.824 --> 00:06:02.047
that I've got to get myself
back on my feet again.

00:06:02.047 --> 00:06:04.648
- What advice do your
parents give you then?

00:06:04.648 --> 00:06:07.272
- They were really honest
to God just cool about it.

00:06:07.272 --> 00:06:08.143
They were parents.

00:06:08.143 --> 00:06:09.874
They just wanted to look after me.

00:06:09.874 --> 00:06:11.469
Live with them for awhile.

00:06:11.469 --> 00:06:14.000
My Dad lent me some money to pay the bank.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:16.746
Then eventually I managed
to pay my Dad back.

00:06:16.746 --> 00:06:19.126
You know it was what any parent would do

00:06:19.126 --> 00:06:23.750
but like I said it wasn't
the worst time of my life.

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:25.971
And actually when I look
back on it now I still smile

00:06:25.971 --> 00:06:30.467
because I've bought this beat
up old British sports car

00:06:30.467 --> 00:06:33.582
worked 18 hours a day and
I was so determined Piers

00:06:33.582 --> 00:06:35.790
just to get it right this time

00:06:35.790 --> 00:06:37.924
'cause I knew I'd got it wrong.

00:06:37.924 --> 00:06:40.408
So now I found it much
easier to deal with things.

00:06:40.408 --> 00:06:43.298
If that wasn't the worst
time of your life what was?

00:06:43.298 --> 00:06:45.652
- When my Dad died was
the worst time in my life

00:06:45.652 --> 00:06:47.537
as you know you and I have discussed that.

00:06:47.537 --> 00:06:50.704
That like getting ill, losing a parent

00:06:51.817 --> 00:06:53.833
or losing somebody close to you,

00:06:53.833 --> 00:06:56.342
that's the worst thing that
could ever happen in your life.

00:06:56.342 --> 00:06:59.071
In all the things that I worry about now,

00:06:59.071 --> 00:07:02.417
just trivial, I mean even
the whole kind of Idol,

00:07:02.417 --> 00:07:04.712
X Factor thing, I laugh abut it

00:07:04.712 --> 00:07:08.685
because it's such a high class problem.

00:07:08.685 --> 00:07:12.195
My show's better than your
show but we both got a show.

00:07:12.195 --> 00:07:14.014
(laughs)

00:07:14.014 --> 00:07:15.365
- It's a bit like Colin
Firth and Hugh Grant

00:07:15.365 --> 00:07:16.587
in the fountain in Bridget Jones.

00:07:16.587 --> 00:07:18.165
- Exactly
- Fighting you know.

00:07:18.165 --> 00:07:19.240
- Over what?

00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:20.351
I mean the fact that we're here,

00:07:20.351 --> 00:07:22.795
we can do this it's fantastic.

00:07:22.795 --> 00:07:24.975
- Do you think the secret to your success

00:07:24.975 --> 00:07:26.791
is actually that you are in many ways

00:07:26.791 --> 00:07:28.258
in the way you lead your life,

00:07:28.258 --> 00:07:29.753
your tastes are quite average,

00:07:29.753 --> 00:07:31.386
middle of the road, the kind of stuff

00:07:31.386 --> 00:07:33.326
the mass market would like?

00:07:33.326 --> 00:07:35.649
- For sure absolutely right.

00:07:35.649 --> 00:07:38.673
And I don't think you can make records,

00:07:38.673 --> 00:07:41.505
I don't think you can
make TV shows properly

00:07:41.505 --> 00:07:44.857
unless you can sit there
as a listener or a viewer.

00:07:44.857 --> 00:07:46.837
You can't guess what people like.

00:07:46.837 --> 00:07:49.327
It'd be like me guessing
what you want for dinner.

00:07:49.327 --> 00:07:50.160
It's impossible.

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:52.162
I can tell you what I want.

00:07:52.162 --> 00:07:54.914
So I base everything on my own tastes

00:07:54.914 --> 00:07:59.519
and I have fairly broad I
would say quite simple tastes.

00:07:59.519 --> 00:08:01.751
I never tried to make my show mean.

00:08:01.751 --> 00:08:04.348
It was supposed to be
funnier rather than mean.

00:08:04.348 --> 00:08:06.036
- Some of you just say
some of the meanest things

00:08:06.036 --> 00:08:07.765
ever seen on American television.

00:08:07.765 --> 00:08:09.665
- No and tell you what,
and coming from you,

00:08:09.665 --> 00:08:11.316
not intentionally but
you know what we're like.

00:08:11.316 --> 00:08:12.824
We have a different sense of humor.

00:08:12.824 --> 00:08:13.657
- [Piers] Yeah.

00:08:13.657 --> 00:08:15.438
- We look at things in a different way.

00:08:15.438 --> 00:08:17.368
I like things to a bit more extreme,

00:08:17.368 --> 00:08:20.932
a bit more crazy and I always
felt with the Idol producers

00:08:20.932 --> 00:08:23.457
they wanted it to be a
bit more homely and safe

00:08:23.457 --> 00:08:24.746
so I think for them now,

00:08:24.746 --> 00:08:26.883
they're very, very happy
'cause they can make the show

00:08:26.883 --> 00:08:28.603
they want to make now and then I can go

00:08:28.603 --> 00:08:29.979
and make the show I want to make.

00:08:29.979 --> 00:08:31.495
- [Piers] Will you be
a different character

00:08:31.495 --> 00:08:33.985
on X Factor as the one we saw on Idol?

00:08:33.985 --> 00:08:35.574
- Like what?

00:08:35.574 --> 00:08:36.658
- Can't imagine it.

00:08:36.658 --> 00:08:39.059
Those other people trust us
this is what he's really like.

00:08:39.059 --> 00:08:40.008
It's not an act.

00:08:40.008 --> 00:08:41.581
- No but I was going to say that

00:08:41.581 --> 00:08:44.720
that i think that you can't
go on one of these show

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:45.964
and put on an act.

00:08:45.964 --> 00:08:50.097
I mean if somebody horrific
is singing in front of you,

00:08:50.097 --> 00:08:52.671
I can't sit there and go
take a few more lessons

00:08:52.671 --> 00:08:54.786
and come back next year
everything's going to be great

00:08:54.786 --> 00:08:57.071
I'm like put this person
out of their misery.

00:08:57.071 --> 00:09:00.488
Or it makes me laugh but I do like things

00:09:01.964 --> 00:09:04.297
to be really, really extreme

00:09:05.537 --> 00:09:08.648
and I like it to be slightly chaotic

00:09:08.648 --> 00:09:10.720
and I like it to be controversial.

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:13.650
- How can I make you feel
important in this interview?

00:09:13.650 --> 00:09:14.900
- You already have you're sitting here.

00:09:14.900 --> 00:09:16.651
I feel important.

00:09:16.651 --> 00:09:18.717
- I noticed when you were
saying that the first thing

00:09:18.717 --> 00:09:21.107
you try to do is convince the other person

00:09:21.107 --> 00:09:22.126
that you're going to
make them feel important.

00:09:22.126 --> 00:09:23.079
- It's good that you mention it.

00:09:23.079 --> 00:09:26.117
It's something my Dad taught
me when I was very young

00:09:26.117 --> 00:09:27.311
and I asked him a question once,

00:09:27.311 --> 00:09:28.891
you know how he runs a company

00:09:28.891 --> 00:09:30.680
and he said it's very simple.

00:09:30.680 --> 00:09:32.690
He said everybody has a sign on their head

00:09:32.690 --> 00:09:34.661
that says make me feel important

00:09:34.661 --> 00:09:36.751
and it's something I've
always been very aware of

00:09:36.751 --> 00:09:40.029
when you make a show and
you've got 150 people on it,

00:09:40.029 --> 00:09:42.908
every person on that
show rightly has to feel

00:09:42.908 --> 00:09:46.444
that they're acknowledged
for the part they play.

00:09:46.444 --> 00:09:48.230
If you start thinking it's all about you,

00:09:48.230 --> 00:09:49.953
you've got a big problem.

00:09:49.953 --> 00:09:51.919
- Sting recently said the X Factor

00:09:51.919 --> 00:09:54.813
is a preposterous show and you have judges

00:09:54.813 --> 00:09:57.112
who have no recognizable talent apart

00:09:57.112 --> 00:09:59.150
from self promotion and
advising them what to wear

00:09:59.150 --> 00:10:01.612
and how to look, it is appalling.

00:10:01.612 --> 00:10:03.878
- Well first of all anybody
who at the age of 60

00:10:03.878 --> 00:10:04.983
calls themselves Sting

00:10:04.983 --> 00:10:07.407
(audience laughs)

00:10:07.407 --> 00:10:08.407
is an idiot.

00:10:11.496 --> 00:10:13.746
I find those kind of people

00:10:15.451 --> 00:10:17.767
who really do live in mansions

00:10:17.767 --> 00:10:19.967
who come from quite humble beginnings

00:10:19.967 --> 00:10:22.496
and they're living in these stately homes

00:10:22.496 --> 00:10:25.338
professing to know
everything about everything

00:10:25.338 --> 00:10:29.522
in the world and what the
contestants may or may not feel,

00:10:29.522 --> 00:10:31.686
they can say that because
they've got everything

00:10:31.686 --> 00:10:33.004
they want in their lives

00:10:33.004 --> 00:10:35.643
but what they're really
saying is anybody else

00:10:35.643 --> 00:10:39.029
who needs a shot shouldn't
be allowed to do it.

00:10:39.029 --> 00:10:40.563
- Have you always lived your life

00:10:40.563 --> 00:10:43.255
with this relentless honesty?

00:10:43.255 --> 00:10:44.532
- I think so yeah.

00:10:44.532 --> 00:10:48.699
I mean when I was young Dan
I got into the music business

00:10:50.253 --> 00:10:53.781
via the mail room and you kind of hustle

00:10:53.781 --> 00:10:57.713
your way into the main
part of the record company

00:10:57.713 --> 00:11:00.445
or the music publishing
companies it was in those days

00:11:00.445 --> 00:11:02.329
and you try and work your way up

00:11:02.329 --> 00:11:04.107
and the interesting
thing is it's a business

00:11:04.107 --> 00:11:07.422
where they invest millions
and millions of dollars

00:11:07.422 --> 00:11:09.349
but they give you no training

00:11:09.349 --> 00:11:13.432
so when you screw up and
I did in the early days,

00:11:14.318 --> 00:11:16.683
I mean I was told in no certain terms

00:11:16.683 --> 00:11:20.850
why I'd made a mistake and
I was taught tough love

00:11:21.876 --> 00:11:24.631
and in return I try and
do the same thing back

00:11:24.631 --> 00:11:26.684
because the worst thing you can do

00:11:26.684 --> 00:11:29.369
is particularly when I do a talent show

00:11:29.369 --> 00:11:33.025
if somebody comes in with
absolutely no talent whatsoever

00:11:33.025 --> 00:11:35.948
and absolutely no chance of a career,

00:11:35.948 --> 00:11:39.620
I think it's a crime
to say to these people

00:11:39.620 --> 00:11:42.009
well take a couple singing
lessons and it will all work out.

00:11:42.009 --> 00:11:44.634
Because it just doesn't work out that way.

00:11:44.634 --> 00:11:46.335
- So let me just go to Susan Boyle

00:11:46.335 --> 00:11:49.539
why was that one of
your favorite auditions?

00:11:49.539 --> 00:11:53.744
- Susan Boyle if I'm being honest with you

00:11:53.744 --> 00:11:56.102
on the day I was in such a bad mood,

00:11:56.102 --> 00:11:59.323
I just didn't want to be there, bored,

00:11:59.323 --> 00:12:00.949
never thought we'd find anyone good,

00:12:00.949 --> 00:12:02.324
the second I saw her come out,

00:12:02.324 --> 00:12:04.268
I see them from the
right hand side walk out

00:12:04.268 --> 00:12:06.215
and I'm like oh my God what we have?

00:12:06.215 --> 00:12:08.989
(laughs)

00:12:08.989 --> 00:12:11.184
- [Oprah] You were judging
the book by its cover.

00:12:11.184 --> 00:12:14.820
- 100 million percent
yes and I also guessed

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:17.138
'cause they're holding the
dreaded hand held microphone,

00:12:17.138 --> 00:12:20.207
it's a singer I know this
is going to be terrible

00:12:20.207 --> 00:12:21.886
and I was completely
judging the book because

00:12:21.886 --> 00:12:24.279
just the way she was walking on I thought

00:12:24.279 --> 00:12:26.987
she's either going to tell
jokes or she's going to sing

00:12:26.987 --> 00:12:29.136
or even worse a combination of the two.

00:12:29.136 --> 00:12:31.609
So I was so not interested.

00:12:31.609 --> 00:12:33.494
And the audience were the same.

00:12:33.494 --> 00:12:34.771
You know they sat through a long time

00:12:34.771 --> 00:12:36.772
they were restless and I
wasn't really listening

00:12:36.772 --> 00:12:40.355
to her answers and then
she started to sing

00:12:42.928 --> 00:12:45.188
and I thought my God
we've to this so wrong

00:12:45.188 --> 00:12:46.988
and then when I watched the clip back

00:12:46.988 --> 00:12:48.557
when it was all edited together,

00:12:48.557 --> 00:12:50.617
and I saw the whole thing

00:12:50.617 --> 00:12:52.928
I actually hated myself.

00:12:52.928 --> 00:12:54.239
I mean genuinely.

00:12:54.239 --> 00:12:55.391
- You hated yourself?

00:12:55.391 --> 00:12:58.949
- Yeah I thought you know
what we're all sneering,

00:12:58.949 --> 00:13:00.011
we're not listening to people.

00:13:00.011 --> 00:13:03.633
We're judging everybody I
just didn't like what I saw.

00:13:03.633 --> 00:13:04.466
- In yourself?

00:13:04.466 --> 00:13:05.299
- For sure.

00:13:05.299 --> 00:13:07.281
Yeah it was a wake up call

00:13:07.281 --> 00:13:10.809
and I was happy that
she made us look stupid.

00:13:10.809 --> 00:13:13.562
Genuinely happy 'cause we deserved it.

00:13:13.562 --> 00:13:15.904
Seriously I was blown away by that

00:13:15.904 --> 00:13:17.973
and I just want to address something.

00:13:17.973 --> 00:13:19.554
I know the show is Britain's Got Talent

00:13:19.554 --> 00:13:20.634
and you're not from Britain

00:13:20.634 --> 00:13:22.838
but I am absolutely honored
that you chose this show

00:13:22.838 --> 00:13:24.751
to show us your talent.

00:13:24.751 --> 00:13:25.846
I really genuinely am.

00:13:25.846 --> 00:13:26.679
(audience cheers)

00:13:26.679 --> 00:13:30.110
I think everybody feels the same way.

00:13:30.110 --> 00:13:32.648
Always welcome talent wherever
it is around the world

00:13:32.648 --> 00:13:34.831
and I genuinely feel privileged

00:13:34.831 --> 00:13:36.906
that we've just seen one
of the most amazing things

00:13:36.906 --> 00:13:41.073
I've ever seen on this show
you really moved me, fantastic.

00:13:43.416 --> 00:13:44.498
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:13:44.498 --> 00:13:48.034
I made this video because
Micheala Lynch asked me too.

00:13:48.034 --> 00:13:49.174
So if there's a famous entrepreneur that

00:13:49.174 --> 00:13:51.353
you want me to profile next
leave it down in the comments

00:13:51.353 --> 00:13:53.436
below and I'll see what I can do.

00:13:53.436 --> 00:13:55.286
I'd also love to know
which of Simon's rules

00:13:55.286 --> 00:13:56.709
had the biggest impact on you.

00:13:56.709 --> 00:13:57.542
What did you learn most?

00:13:57.542 --> 00:13:59.750
What are you going to apply
to your life for business?

00:13:59.750 --> 00:14:02.458
What was most eye
opening about this video?

00:14:02.458 --> 00:14:04.276
Leave it in the comments
and I'm going to join

00:14:04.276 --> 00:14:05.318
in the discussion.

00:14:05.318 --> 00:14:06.377
I also want to give a quick shout out

00:14:06.377 --> 00:14:09.147
to Dorma Jean McGruder thank you so much

00:14:09.147 --> 00:14:10.456
for picking up a copy of my book.

00:14:10.456 --> 00:14:12.724
It really, really means a lot to me.

00:14:12.724 --> 00:14:15.633
So thank you guys again for
watching I believe in you.

00:14:15.633 --> 00:14:17.934
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:14:17.934 --> 00:14:20.029
and whatever your one word is.

00:14:20.029 --> 00:14:22.362
Much love I'll see you soon.

00:14:28.711 --> 00:14:30.271
- If you really want to get good,

00:14:30.271 --> 00:14:32.197
you're the first in and
you're the last out.

00:14:32.197 --> 00:14:35.049
And you listen rather than talking

00:14:35.049 --> 00:14:37.710
because if your lucky in life

00:14:37.710 --> 00:14:39.312
and your eyes and ears are open,

00:14:39.312 --> 00:14:43.469
you may bump into somebody
who can transform your life

00:14:43.469 --> 00:14:46.401
'cause they can teach you
but to meet that person

00:14:46.401 --> 00:14:48.916
you got to be receptive
if that makes sense.

00:14:48.916 --> 00:14:52.169
To do what I do the only thing

00:14:52.169 --> 00:14:56.658
that bothers me is whether or
not I'm relevant any longer

00:14:56.658 --> 00:14:59.354
because I think if you're
sitting on these shows

00:14:59.354 --> 00:15:01.728
but you're not having hit
records or hit artists

00:15:01.728 --> 00:15:05.116
in your real life I think
you look a bit of an idiot

00:15:05.116 --> 00:15:09.509
so that's all I really care
about Dan is that I can sit

00:15:09.509 --> 00:15:12.902
on one of these shows
and justify why I'm there

00:15:12.902 --> 00:15:16.902
because in my day job I
am creating hit artists.

00:15:17.950 --> 00:15:19.845
Otherwise you're just a judge for hire

00:15:19.845 --> 00:15:21.735
which I just couldn't do.

